<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jacmel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1698">1698</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jacmel is a port city on the south coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, founded by the French in 1698 and now capital of the Sud-Est <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>. In the nineteenth century it was one of the country's main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>-exporting ports, and its merchant houses with iron balconies survive. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> sailed from Jacmel in December 1816 with Haitian help on his expedition to liberate Venezuela.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jacmel.jpg" alt="Jacmel" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Doron · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JacmelView.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Coffee remained the main export, shipped from Port-au-Prince, <b>Jacmel</b> and Les Cayes to European and American merchants.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti-1806-1849">Republic of Haiti (1806-1849) · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
